face-image: img/redfront.jpg

= American Rescue Plan

The Covid-19 pandemic has plunged America into economic recession. Tens of millions of Americans are at risk of eviction, unemployment, and are food insecure. Even Donald Trump recognized the need for direct aid to the American people, in the CARES act of March 2020. A kew promise on the campaign trail for Bernie Sanders was the passing of an expanded stimulus package that would give $2000 checks to Americans.

Now, the bill is finally being debated in the Senate. Our majorities are razor thin, with a single digit majority in the house and a tied senate. Moderate <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Democrats**</span> like Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema are calling for the administration to seek a compromise with Republicans on this issue. With them being king-makers in the senate, we definitely won't be able to have as expansive of a bill as we'd wish, but if it comes down to it we can most likely convince Sinema and Manchin to abandon their efforts for a bipartisan compromise with Republicans in favor for a slightly more progressive bill that will be passed through the budget appropriation process.

